european leaders arriving in brussels  for a crucial meeting on ukraine  at the start of the crisis they had  surprised many with unity and strong  sanctions  but a sixth package of measures  including an oil ban has been stalled  for nearly a month some expressed  optimism the deadlock can be broken  there is high chances of being passed  through  and  the unity should be maintained in the  european council as we have done up to  now so that's important to continue but  such an upbeat assessment was soon  dashed by the eu leader with the  friendliest relations with vladimir  putin hungary's recently re-elected  prime minister victor orban has for  weeks been the one to oppose the oil ban  to win him over it's now being proposed  that to begin with the ban will only  apply to oil that comes on ships from  russia not supplies that are delivered  by pipeline  but mr orban told reporters there was no  deal yet means you know that we are in a  very difficult situation  basically because of the irresponsible  behavior of the commission  the head of the commission didn't  address such criticism but she did  suggest there wouldn't be a breakthrough  during this meeting we are not there yet  but it has matured for sure  my expectations are low that it will be  solved in the next 48 hours but i'm  confident that thereafter there will be  a possibility the european leaders were  joined by video link from kiev by  president zielenski a show of unity and  solidarity for ukraine but all that is  rather overshadowed by the continuing  impasse on sanctions  james bay's al jazeera brussels  ukraine's foreign minister says keeve is  for state frustrated by the slow pace of  negotiations  you know well how we are tiring ukraine  of some special decisions half measures  special approaches some models which  some european capitals are constantly  inventing for ukraine we need clear  legal confirmation that ukraine is part  of the european integration project and  such confirmation will be giving ukraine  the status of candidates  meanwhile a french journalist has been  killed by russian shelling in ukraine  frederick leclerc  was a reporter with bfm tv  the french foreign minister who is in  ukraine has called investigation into  his killing in luhansk  the french president emmanuel macron  also paid tribute by tweeting the  following federic le clercymoth was in  ukraine to show the reality of the war  he was aboard a humanitarian bus  alongside civilians forced to flee to  escape russian bombs and he was fatally  shot  saying basravi is in kiev he has more on  the circumstances surrounding the french  journalists death  we started hearing reports of a  journalist that had been killed just a  few hours ago and now we can name him uh  french president emmanuel macron has uh  named the gentleman who was  killed earlier today and his name is  frederick leclerc imhoff and the  circumstances of his death are are  tragic it has to be said um he was  accompanying a a rescue effort in the  luhansk region we're not sure exactly  where as yet it hasn't been confirmed  but we are hearing some social media  chatter to suggest that it was  evacuations being carried out from the  city of several donetsk which has been  under siege by russian forces for some  time  during that rescue operation the head of  the luhansk region made a statement  about the incident that happened he says  that the evacuation has been officially  stopped russians fired at a car that was  going to pick up people a french  journalist was killed an armored  evacuation vehicle was picking up 10  people from the area  that came under enemy fire shrapnel and  shells pierced the armored vehicle  that was being used in a fatal wound to  the neck was received by this accredited  french journalist who was covering the  evacuation at the time so certainly a  very tragic incident it brings the death  toll  of journalists  in this conflict since the russian  invasion began to approximately 24 that  is the latest figure we have pales in  comparison it has to be said to the 9  000 estimated civilian deaths that have  taken place some estimates put that  number much higher but it does indicate  how difficult in danger it is to report  this war it's only been going on since  february  let's take a quick look at the state of  playing ukraine's front lines at the  moment the areas in red are controlled  by russia and its separatist allies the  governor of luhan says russian forces  have entered the city of several donetsk  there's also heavy fighting in these  areas as ukrainian troops hold back  russian attempts to encircle forces in  donetsk  it's a different story in the south  where ukraine has managed to launch a  counter-offensive to recapture kessan  we can speak now to hannah celeste  who is a security studies program  director at the foreign policy and  security think tank ukrainian prison she  joins us from odessa in ukraine thank  you for speaking to us on the programme  the focus of the battle as we're hearing  uh in in uh there earlier in is in the  east right now and  the russians appear to be throwing  everything they have at it they do hold  an overwhelming advantage when it comes  to weapons and firepower how long do you  think the ukrainians can hold them off  as things stand  definitely the last week the fightings  intensified significantly on the east  front uh that is mostly in the lugansk  and the nets regions and it is very  difficult to say now uh what will be the  exact time that ukraine can  uh stay like this because we can't say  that russians really advanced in some  territories they advanced more like in  lugansk in some territories they've been  advancing and then thrown back so the  fighting is really just in the meat of  what is happening at the same time this  week and the next week ukraine is  expecting very significant supply of the  heavy weapons that's been already  announced by the united states of  america the last week the 4 billion  supports being signed by the president  and over there we are expecting that  type of the weapons that ukraine really  needs now for the counter fans that's  multi-launch rocket systems that the uh  drones uh the combat drones that ukraine  can use over there and the supply of  this weapons weapons can significantly  change the operative situation  what are you actually hearing about  whether you are going to get these  weapons and when because we've heard  keeve express frustration  uh again and again on on the slow pace  uh of a variety of things for example  the sanction talks uh and and demanding  more weapons do you feel that europe and  the us is addressing your concerns  i would not unite all countries together  because we have really very different  situation uh some countries even like  the united states of america supplying  the weapons very often in 72 hours from  the decision made to the actual being  already in the unit on the ground the  question is that when you supply  javelins it can be done easier than when  you supply the multi-launch rocket  system you just imagine the sizes and  the logistics especially in the times of  war at the same time we definitely have  the problems like with germany when uh  it's been announced more than a month  ago that they're supplying us with the  unnecessary armored verticals and we are  still expecting them on the ground  at the same time lithuania where's tony  is supplying as soon as possible what  they have and even australia the latest  decisions about the uh uh the kyles that  we received from them we received them  literally in four days from  announcements and you can imagine what  are the logistics from australia to  ukraine so i would not say that this  frustration is general against all the  partners it's definitely against the  individual partners and ukraine  understands that we need as soon as  possible everything that's why we are in  such a demanding mood because each day  it means that each day of the suffering  of people on the ground  we're hearing the term  war of attrition being used to describe  russia's strategy in parts of the east  what is your understanding of that term  and what do you think the logic is  behind russia's tactics  definitely you could see it for the last  three months especially in towns like  mariupoli or harkid that russia is  trying just to destroy everything what  is there there are several reasons for  this first of all is to bring fear for  people on the ground so uh the people  would not support any of the military  actions and  those who can oppose on the ground like  guerrillas a partisan they would be just  leaving the territory because they  understand the uh severe actions and  consequences the same is for journalists  journalists are usually not allowed to  be there where they have very heavy  fightings are happening for their  security uh reasons but also because the  russian federation currently um  demonstrated that their armed forces are  not so strong as they promoted them so  on the north they were not able to have  a close fight and they started to use  the last months as much as possible the  long-range missiles that are very  destructive you can imagine the furore  again abroad or others uh what are the  consequences of these missiles and they  also use a lot in eastern ukraine the uh  turmeric missiles and the cluster  missiles so those that most of the  countries even prohibited for the use  especially in the residential areas but  in this way they cannot come even to the  territory of ukraine they can be very  safe at their roman territory but  demolish as much as possible of the  civilian infrastructure  can you tell us a bit more about what is  happening in kerason because we're  hearing that ukraine has launched a  counter offensive there  how how how are they doing in pushing  russia back  first of all it's necessary to  understand that her song region is very  long it's maybe not so big but it is  very long because it is covering uh the  whole territory both in azov and in uh  the blacks here just uh above the crania  so the situation is very different on  the different parts of these uh  frontline however definitely because her  stone been under the occupation since  the beginning of march and it is very  difficult situation in terms of human  rights violations over there russians  already arrested more than 500 people  only in the city of herson where the  demonstration in march and april being  almost daily of the i mean pro-ukrainian  demonstration demonstrations against the  uh russian occupied forces over there so  as for now ukraine is trying to get  village by village road by road a little  bit of territory is coming closer to the  city of herzone to have the possibility  for the counter attack and but also to  minimize the possibilities of the attack  against mikko live city the next one  after his own to the west as for now  it's really step by step very slowly  because the biggest forces are  concentrated on the east kirsten  probably will be the next region  assuming the situation on the east will  stabilize a little bit more  hannah i really appreciate you speaking  to us on al jazeera hannah celeste  security studies and program director at  the ukrainian prison

